      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wellston was named for Erastus Wells, a pioneer who helped establish the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wellston%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Wellston incorporated in 1909, dissolved after three years due to government difficulties, then reestablished as a city in 1949.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wellston%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the early 1900s, the Wagner Electric Company built a factory on Plymouth Avenue, creating 4,500 jobs during World War I.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wellston%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "North of the Wagner site, ABEX Corporation opened a steel foundry in 1923, which operated until 1982.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wellston%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wellston hosts a MetroLink station with 243 park‑and‑ride spaces, linking the city to St. Louis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
